        "problem": "Evaluate $(\\frac{30}{27}).$",
        "worked_solution": "First, note that $(\\frac{30}{27})$ means the same thing as ${}_{30}{C}_{27}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=30$ and $r=27,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{30}{C}_{27} & =\\frac{30!}{(30-27)!27!} \\\\  & =\\frac{30!}{3!27!} \\\\  & =\\frac{30\\cdot 29\\cdot 28\\cdot \\cancel{27!}}{3!\\cancel{27!}} \\\\  & =4\\,060.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
